<!--
  navigate_to_pose_tricycle_v3_rpp.xml - the STAGE/APPROACH variant.
  G5 TASK 7, and the owner's ruling of 2026-09-01.

  READ THE OTHER FILE FIRST. This tree is
  behavior_trees/navigate_to_pose_tricycle_v3.xml with ONE ATTRIBUTE
  CHANGED, and every argument in that file's header - why there is a
  tree here at all, why Spin and BackUp are out, why the navigation
  budget is where it is, what DirectionStablePath does and why it wraps
  what it wraps - holds here unaltered and is not repeated.

  THE ONE ATTRIBUTE:

      <ControllerSelector ... default_controller="FollowPathRPP" .../>

  against `"FollowPath"` in the primary. tests/test_nav2_params.py
  parses BOTH files and asserts that the element trees are otherwise
  IDENTICAL - same tags, same order, same attributes - so a change made
  to one and forgotten in the other is a test failure and not a
  divergence somebody finds on a rig.

  ---- WHY A SECOND FILE AND NOT A TOPIC ----

  nav2 offers two ways to put a second controller on a leg and this
  track uses the one with no clock in it.

    THE TOPIC. `ControllerSelector` subscribes to `controller_selector`
      and writes what it receives onto the blackboard. Selecting that
      way means publishing a string and then sending a goal, and the two
      race: the BT ticks at bt_loop_duration and the FollowPath action
      node re-sends its action goal when `controller_id` changes, so a
      message that lands late does not select a controller for the run -
      it CHANGES controller in the middle of one. Nothing on this stack
      publishes to that topic and nothing here starts.
    THIS FILE. `NavigateToPose` carries a `behavior_tree` field.
      bt_navigator loads the tree named there for that goal and caches
      it by filename, so the selection is made ONCE, BEFORE the goal is
      accepted, by the same call that sets the pose. There is no window
      in which the wrong controller can be running.

  THE SELECTOR STAYS IN THE TREE, both here and in the primary, and its
  default is what each file's name says. That is exactly what the
  primary's header said the pair was kept for: "they are how a later
  task adds a second controller without editing this file".

  ---- WHICH LEGS RUN THIS, AND config.yaml OWNS THE ANSWER ----

  nav.cases.<case>.controller. `rpp` resolves to nav.bt_xml_rpp, which
  is this file; `mppi` resolves to nav.bt_xml, which is the primary; a
  case with no key at all gets the primary, so this file drives nothing
  it was not named for. tools/drive_goal.py puts the resolved path on
  the goal and RECORDS it in the session, beside the tree's own md5,
  because a run behind a different tree that wore the same label would
  be tabled with runs it has nothing to do with.

  ---- WHAT THE CONTROLLER ITSELF IS ----

  nav2.yaml, controller_server, `FollowPathRPP`. The short version:
  MPPI's measured failure at an adverse entry heading is its own prior
  winning a softmax after the path critics have gated themselves off,
  and RegulatedPurePursuit has no sampling, no critics and no prior for
  that to happen to. The long version, with the source it was read out
  of, is in that block.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Timeout msec="335000" name="NavigationBudget">
      <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="6" name="NavigateRecovery">
        <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
          <ControllerSelector selected_controller="{selected_controller}" default_controller="FollowPathRPP" topic_name="controller_selector"/>
          <PlannerSelector selected_planner="{selected_planner}" default_planner="GridBased" topic_name="planner_selector"/>
          <RateController hz="1.0">
            <DirectionStablePath path="{path}" odom_topic="/m5v3/odometry/filtered" hold_speed="0.05" hold_all="false" hold_max_s="10.0" consume_floor_m="0.3">
              <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
                <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="{selected_planner}" error_code_id="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
                <Sequence>
                  <WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp error_code="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
                  <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
                </Sequence>
              </RecoveryNode>
            </DirectionStablePath>
          </RateController>
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
            <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="{selected_controller}" error_code_id="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
            <Sequence>
              <WouldAControllerRecoveryHelp error_code="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            </Sequence>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </PipelineSequence>
        <Sequence>
          <Fallback>
            <WouldAControllerRecoveryHelp error_code="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
            <WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp error_code="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
          </Fallback>
          <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
            <GoalUpdated/>
            <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
              <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
                <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
                <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
              </Sequence>
              <Wait wait_duration="5.0"/>
            </RoundRobin>
          </ReactiveFallback>
        </Sequence>
      </RecoveryNode>
    </Timeout>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
